  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 217 AM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nThe 00Z RAOB data show the H5 ridge holding over the southern and\ncentral Plains with weak flow directly overhead and southwest flow\ndisplaced over the Intermountain West into the northern Rockies.\nThe Pacific Northwest trough and associated upper low remain the\nprimary features of consequence upstream, with tropical moisture\nremaining along the Gulf coast. At the surface, a quasi-stationary\nboundary sits north of the area in Nebraska with lee troughing to\nthe west, mainly over central into western Kansas. The entire CWA\nremains under the heat dome once again today. On Wednesday, heat\nindices touched the 103 to 106 range around midday before deep\nmixing scoured afternoon dewpoints into the low 60s, trimming heat\nindices back toward the air temperature by late afternoon. The NBM\ncontinues to run both too cool on highs and too moist on afternoon\ndewpoints. This has been taken into account for this forecast\ncycle as well.\n\nEarly this morning, weak ascent along the 305 K isentropic surface\nover north central Kansas may squeeze out a few showers. Forecast\nsoundings keep a deep dry layer below cloud bases, so anything\nthat falls fights sub-cloud evaporation and coverage stays isolated\nat best. Chances hold below 15 percent and end by mid morning,\nwith most locations staying dry and any measurable rainfall\nconfined to a few spots.\n\nThe heat headline driver remains focused on the dewpoint question\nand the depth of adiabatic mixing, and the HREF ensemble members\nsuggest this as well. Thursday afternoon dewpoint spread at Topeka\nruns from near 60 in the drier members to near 70 in the moister\nones, and that range is the difference between heat indices near\n100 and heat indices in the middle 100s. The drier solutions have\nverified each afternoon by mid afternoon, though not before heat\nindices reached advisory criteria around midday, and that midday\nwindow is what the Heat Advisory now in effect through Saturday\nevening covers. Beyond Saturday, southerly flow persists and the\nensemble mean keeps afternoon dewpoints in the middle 60s with H85\ntemperatures holding in the middle 20s C, so Sunday and Labor Day\nsit on the advisory fence as well. If the deeper mixing keeps\nverifying, criteria likely remain marginal; if the moistening\ntrend wins, an extension through the full weekend will likely win\nout. Either way the heat itself is slow to recede: highs in the\nupper 90s to near 100, warmest north central where mixing depths\nare deepest, with overnight lows in the middle and upper 70s\nkeeping record warm minimums in play through Saturday morning.\n\nThe ridge holds through the holiday weekend and into early next\nweek. Ensemble data suggest the mid level heights over the area\nstay elevated with tight member agreement through Tuesday, and WPC\ncluster analysis keeps positive height anomalies over the central\nUS in every cluster into the middle of next week. Slight rain\nchances along the Nebraska border early next week most likely\nreflect the growing spread rather than an organized signal. The\nreal change appears to arrive late next week. Ensemble heights\nfall sharply around Thursday as the Pacific trough finally ejects\ninto the Plains, H85 temperatures step down several degrees C at\nthe same time, and the cluster favoring that evolution is weighted\ntoward the ECMWF membership. That system may bring the first\nmeaningful rain chances in two weeks and the first stretch of near\nnormal temperatures since around the third week of August.\nConfidence in the pattern change is increasing slightly based on\nthe most recent trends; confidence in the exact day remains low.",
